{\pard\qc\b\fs48 The Lantern Keeper\par}
{\pard\qc\fs28 by Ada Marsh\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Book One: The Coast\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Chapter 1: The Harbour\par}
{\pard\fs24 The harbour was quiet before the boats went out, and the gulls stood on the posts like people waiting for a bus.\par}
{\pard\fs24 Nell walked the length of the sea wall twice, counting the lamps, and then sat on the steps to watch the tide come in.\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Chapter 2: The Storm\par}
{\pard\fs24 The storm came up from the south in the afternoon, grey at first and then the colour of slate.\par}
{\pard\fs24 By supper the windows rattled in their frames, and the fire leaned sideways in the grate whenever the door was opened.\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Book Two: The Hills\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Chapter 3: The Gate\par}
{\pard\fs24 The orchard behind the house had seven trees, and each one had a name that only the family used.\par}
{\pard\fs24 The well in the yard had been dry for years, but the bucket still hung over it on its rope.\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Chapter 4: The Orchard\par}
{\pard\fs24 The stair to the attic creaked on every third step, so that anyone climbing it played the same slow tune.\par}
{\pard\fs24 Her uncle kept the lighthouse on the point, and every evening he climbed the hundred and twelve steps with a can of oil.\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Volume III: The River\par}
{\pard\b\fs36 Chapter 5: The Bridge\par}
{\pard\fs24 Nobody slept much that night. In the morning the beach was covered in weed and broken crates and one very surprised crab.\par}
{\pard\fs24 The light turned all night, as it always had, though no ship came close enough to need it.\par}
